.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt sledger 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sledger
.Nd sledger journal
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This is the file format used by sledger. It is intentionally minimal.

A journal is a plaintext file consists of a sequence of postings. A posting
is essentially one transaction. A posting begins with a line containing the
date and description. The line begins with the date in YYYY-MM-DD format. The
date is followed by whitespace. The first non-whitespace character after that
starts the description, which takes the rest of the line.

The remaining lines in a posting begin with a tab and specify the amounts
being moved between accounts. After the tab is the name of the account. The
account name music start with a letter, but the rest of it can contain
alphanumeric characters along with a colon or an underscore. The colon is
used as a delimiter to allow placing accounts into a hierarchy. After the
account comes some more whitespace, followed by a decimal number, some more
whitespace, and the unit name. The unit name must start with a letter, but
the rest can be an alphanumeric character or an underscore.

Optionally, one of these lines can be blank and its value inferred by adding
up the rest of the lines in the transaction. This only works if all values in
the transaction are of the same unit.

.Sh EXAMPLE
.Bd -literal
2026-05-03 Groceries
	expenses:groceries	-43.35 USD
	liabilities:cc	43.35 USD

2026-06-15 2x8GB of DDR4 RAM
	expenses:ram	-50,003.38 USD
	liabilities:cc
.Ed

